n- Mildly elevated specific gravity\n\n**Basic labs**\n- Creatinine normal\n- eGFR normal\n- White blood cell count normal\n- CRP not elevated\n\n**Renal ultrasound**\n- Mild right-sided hydronephrosis\n- Possible small proximal ureteral stone, not directly seen\n- No renal mass seen\n\n## Preliminary interpretation\nThis synthetic pattern is **compatible with a possible small right ureteral stone causing mild urinary obstruction**.\n\nThat interpretation is supported by:\n- right flank discomfort,\n- trace blood in the urine,\n- and mild right hydronephrosis on ultrasound.\n\nAt the same time, uncertainty remains because:\n- the stone was **not directly visualized**,\n- the pain is not the classic severe colicky pattern,\n- and the available data does not rule out every alternative explanation.\n\n## What it would mean in plain language\nIn this synthetic example, the overall picture suggests a **small stone may be partly blocking urine flow on the right side**, which could explain the discomfort and nausea.\n\nMore reassuring points in the synthetic output:\n- kidney function looks preserved,\n- there is no strong sign of infection,\n- and no mass was seen on ultrasound.\n\n## Suggested next steps\nIf this were a real case, reasonable next steps would include:\n- clinical follow-up if symptoms persist or worsen,\n- urgent assessment if fever, vomiting, severe pain, inability to urinate, visible blood in urine, or feeling acutely unwell develop,\n- and possibly more definitive imaging if uncertainty remains.\n\n**Final note:** this was a **successful synthetic distillation test**, and the result is a **preliminary interpretation only**, not a confirmed diagnosis.\n\nIf you want, I can now do one of these next:\n1. prepare a **second synthetic test** with a different condition pattern, or  \n2. run the same distillation flow using **real conversation context**.","textSignature":"{\"v\":1,\"id\":\"msg_07f5ee05931c6d410169c41817e7548191b77be4967e862298\",\"phase\":\"f